{{also|dish washer}}
==English==
{{wp}}
[[Image:Dishwasher open for loading.jpg|thumb|'''Dishwasher''', open and loaded with dishes.]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|dysche wescheare}}, equivalent to {{af|en|dish|washer}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪʃˌwɒʃə/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪʃˌwɑʃɚ/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-dishwasher.oga|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 dishwasher.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[machine]] for washing dishes.
#: {{ux|en|load the '''dishwasher'''}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Karen Savage|title=Instructions Not Included: The Adventures of New Motherhood
|passage=Plus, there are about a thousand things that also could, or should get done in that time: a load of laundry, loading the '''dishwasher''', tidying the kitchen, picking up a gagillion toys and blankies off the ground, restocking the change tables.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2016-02-12|author=|title=The Black Yeast Exophiala dermatitidis and Other Selected Opportunistic Human Fungal Pathogens Spread from Dishwashers to Kitchens|journal=PLOS ONE|url=http://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0148166|doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0148166|volume=|issue=|pages=
|passage=Conditions inside '''dishwashers''' not only enable selected fungal species to survive, but also promote the selective enrichment of particular polyextremotolerant fungi that can tolerate low and high pH, temperatures up to 60°C or 80°C, occasional dehydration and high organic loads, high concentrations of NaCl, and mechanical stress due to water ejectors.}}
# Someone who washes dishes, especially one [[hired]] to wash dishes in a [[restaurant]].
#: {{syn|en|dishy<q:informal, Australia>}}
#: {{cot|en|bottlewasher}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=Thomas Quealy|title=M + M
|passage=The chefs never cooked before, the cashiers never cashiered before, the '''dishwashers''' never dishwashed before, the waitresses never waitressed before, and the managers never managed before.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|dialect|Wiltshire|Cornwall}} A [[European]] [[bird]], the [[wagtail]].
#* '''2022''', John Coulson Tregarthen, ''Wild Life at the Land's End''
#*: The country-people say that the "tinner," that is the "'''dishwasher'''" or water-wagtail, is scarcer than it was before the blizzard, which must have caused the death of tens of thousands of birds. They call it the tinner, because it builds its nest in the mouth of the old mine-shafts.
# {{lb|en|AU}} A bird, the [[restless flycatcher]] ({{taxlink|Myiagra inquieta|species}}).

